<body>

<header>
  <h1>Active Satellites in Earth Orbit</h1>
  <p class="subtitle">2026 Catalog Analysis — A data-driven assessment of the 14,875 active satellites currently in Earth orbit, their orbital distribution, constellation composition, and launch trends.</p>
  <p class="meta">Data source: CelesTrak / NORAD TLE catalog &nbsp;|&nbsp; Epoch range: 2026-02-26 to 2026-03-30 &nbsp;|&nbsp; Generated: April 2026</p>
</header>

<div class="container">

  <!-- KPI Strip -->
  <div class="kpi-strip">
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-value">14,875</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Total Active Satellites</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-value">86.5%</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">LEO Share</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-value">10,083</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Starlink Constellation</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-value">53.16°</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Median Inclination</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-value">13,461</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Launched Since 2020</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Section 1: Catalog Overview -->
  <section id="overview">
    <h2>Catalog Overview &amp; Key Figures</h2>
    <p>The CelesTrak active-satellite catalog contains <strong>14,875 operational spacecraft</strong> as of late March 2026. Orbital elements span epoch dates from February 26 to March 30, 2026, confirming the catalog is a near-real-time snapshot of the active fleet. The dataset covers everything from 1960s-era calibration spheres still transmitting data to the latest Starlink V2 Mini satellites deployed in early 2026.</p>

    <h3>Statistical Summary</h3>
    <table>
      <tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Min</th><th>Median</th><th>Mean</th><th>Max</th></tr>
      <tr><td>Mean Motion (rev/day)</td><td class="num">0.28</td><td class="num">15.27</td><td class="num">13.42</td><td class="num">16.30</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Inclination (°)</td><td class="num">0.00</td><td class="num">53.16</td><td class="num">60.04</td><td class="num">149.64</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Eccentricity</td><td class="num">0.0000</td><td class="num">0.0002</td><td class="num">0.0018</td><td class="num">0.8957</td></tr>
    </table>

    <div class="callout">
      <strong>Key Takeaway:</strong> The median mean motion of 15.27 rev/day indicates that a typical active satellite today is in LEO at roughly 550 km altitude — a direct consequence of Starlink's fleet, which orbits near that altitude and comprises 67.8% of all active satellites.
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 2: Orbital Regime Classification -->
  <section id="orbit-regime">
    <h2>Orbital Regime Classification</h2>

    <div class="methodology">
      <strong>Classification Method:</strong> Orbital regimes are assigned using MEAN_MOTION thresholds: <strong>LEO</strong> &gt; 14.0 rev/day (period &lt; 103 min, altitude roughly &lt; 2,000 km); <strong>GEO</strong> between 0.9 and 1.1 rev/day (~35,786 km); <strong>MEO</strong> between 1.1 and 14.0 rev/day; <strong>HEO</strong> assigned to objects in the MEO/other band with eccentricity &gt; 0.25 (highly elliptical orbits such as Molniya).
    </div>

    <div class="chart-row">
      <div class="chart-box">
        <h4>Orbital Regime Distribution</h4>
        <canvas id="regimeDonut" height="280"></canvas>
      </div>
      <div class="chart-box">
        <h4>Regime Counts</h4>
        <table>
          <tr><th>Regime</th><th class="num">Count</th><th class="num">Share</th></tr>
          <tr><td>LEO</td><td class="num">12,860</td><td class="num">86.5%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>MEO</td><td class="num">1,408</td><td class="num">9.5%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>GEO</td><td class="num">596</td><td class="num">4.0%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>HEO</td><td class="num">35</td><td class="num">0.2%</td></tr>
        </table>
        <p style="margin-top:16px;">LEO overwhelmingly dominates the active fleet. The 596 GEO satellites — many of them high-value communications and weather platforms — represent just 4.0% of the count but serve a disproportionate share of global communications, broadcast, and meteorological coverage. MEO hosts navigation constellations (GPS, Galileo, BeiDou, GLONASS) and broadband systems like O3b/SES.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
      <strong>Starlink Effect:</strong> Removing Starlink's 10,083 LEO satellites would reduce the LEO population to 2,777, dropping LEO's share from 86.5% to 57.9%. A single operator accounts for more than three-quarters of all LEO objects.
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 3: Constellation Breakdown -->
  <section id="constellation-analysis">
    <h2>Major Constellation &amp; Operator Breakdown</h2>
    <p>Constellations are identified by parsing the <code>OBJECT_NAME</code> prefix. The top 12 identifiable groupings account for 11,372 satellites (76.4% of the catalog).</p>

    <div class="chart-row">
      <div class="chart-box">
        <h4>Top Constellations by Active Satellite Count</h4>
        <canvas id="constellationBar" height="340"></canvas>
      </div>
      <div class="chart-box">
        <h4>Fleet Composition</h4>
        <canvas id="constellationPie" height="340"></canvas>
      </div>
    </div>

    <table>
      <tr><th>Constellation / Group</th><th class="num">Count</th><th class="num">Share</th><th>Regime</th></tr>
      <tr><td>Starlink (SpaceX)</td><td class="num">10,083</td><td class="num">67.8%</td><td>LEO</td></tr>
      <tr><td>OneWeb</td><td class="num">651</td><td class="num">4.4%</td><td>LEO</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Kuiper (Amazon)</td><td class="num">210</td><td class="num">1.4%</td><td>LEO</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Yaogan (China)</td><td class="num">165</td><td class="num">1.1%</td><td>LEO/MEO</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Hulianwang (China SatNet)</td><td class="num">159</td><td class="num">1.1%</td><td>LEO</td></tr>
      <tr><td>COSMOS (Russia)</td><td class="num">126</td><td class="num">0.8%</td><td>Various</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Flock / Dove (Planet Labs)</td><td class="num">119</td><td class="num">0.8%</td><td>LEO</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Qianfan (China)</td><td class="num">108</td><td class="num">0.7%</td><td>LEO</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Iridium NEXT</td><td class="num">80</td><td class="num">0.5%</td><td>LEO</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Lemur (Spire)</td><td class="num">76</td><td class="num">0.5%</td><td>LEO</td></tr>
      <tr><td>GeeSAT</td><td class="num">63</td><td class="num">0.4%</td><td>LEO</td></tr>
      <tr><td>BeiDou (China Nav)</td><td class="num">55</td><td class="num">0.4%</td><td>MEO/GEO</td></tr>
      <tr><td>GPS / Navstar (USA)</td><td class="num">39</td><td class="num">0.3%</td><td>MEO</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Galileo (EU)</td><td class="num">31</td><td class="num">0.2%</td><td>MEO</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Other (&lt;25 each)</td><td class="num">2,910</td><td class="num">19.6%</td><td>Various</td></tr>
    </table>

    <div class="callout">
      <strong>Concentration Risk:</strong> Starlink alone represents 67.8% of all active satellites. The top three constellations (Starlink, OneWeb, Kuiper) account for 73.6%. This extreme concentration means that operational decisions, failures, or deorbit campaigns by a single operator can materially reshape the entire orbital environment.
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 4: Launch Year Trends (MOST IMPORTANT) -->
  <section id="launch-trends">
    <h2>Launch Year Trends</h2>
    <p>Launch year is extracted from the first four characters of <code>OBJECT_ID</code> (the international designator). This represents the year the satellite was <em>launched</em>, not when it became operational. Note that the 2026 figure (962) is partial — covering launches through late March 2026 only.</p>

    <div class="chart-row full">
      <div class="chart-box">
        <h4>Annual Launches of Currently Active Satellites (2000–2026)</h4>
        <canvas id="launchBarChart" height="300"></canvas>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="chart-row full">
      <div class="chart-box">
        <h4>Cumulative Active Fleet Growth (2000–2026)</h4>
        <canvas id="cumulativeChart" height="300"></canvas>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3>The Inflection Point: 2020 Onward</h3>
    <p>The data reveals a dramatic inflection in orbital population growth starting in 2020. In the six years from 2020 through March 2026, <strong>13,461 currently active satellites were launched</strong> — representing <strong>90.5%</strong> of the entire active fleet. The trajectory is unmistakable:</p>

    <table>
      <tr><th>Year</th><th class="num">Launched (Active)</th><th class="num">Year-over-Year Growth</th><th class="num">Cumulative Total</th></tr>
      <tr><td>2018</td><td class="num">200</td><td class="num">+47%</td><td class="num">1,263</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2019</td><td class="num">151</td><td class="num">−25%</td><td class="num">1,414</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2020</td><td class="num">498</td><td class="num">+230%</td><td class="num">1,912</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2021</td><td class="num">1,063</td><td class="num">+113%</td><td class="num">2,975</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2022</td><td class="num">1,804</td><td class="num">+70%</td><td class="num">4,779</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2023</td><td class="num">2,328</td><td class="num">+29%</td><td class="num">7,107</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2024</td><td class="num">2,477</td><td class="num">+6%</td><td class="num">9,584</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2025</td><td class="num">4,329</td><td class="num">+75%</td><td class="num">13,913</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2026 (partial)</td><td class="num">962</td><td class="num">—</td><td class="num">14,875</td></tr>
    </table>

    <div class="callout">
      <strong>2025 was the breakout year:</strong> With 4,329 currently active satellites launched, 2025 alone added more satellites than existed in the entire catalog before 2022. If 2026 maintains this pace, the active fleet could exceed 18,000 by year-end. The acceleration is overwhelmingly driven by Starlink deployment, supplemented by OneWeb, Kuiper, and Chinese broadband constellations.
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 5: Orbital Parameter Deep Dive -->
  <section id="orbital-characteristics">
    <h2>Orbital Parameter Deep Dive</h2>

    <h3>Inclination Distribution</h3>
    <p>Orbital inclination reveals the preferred orbital planes. Constellation operators cluster their satellites at specific inclinations to maximize coverage geometry.</p>

    <div class="chart-row full">
      <div class="chart-box">
        <h4>Inclination Distribution — All Satellites vs. Starlink</h4>
        <canvas id="inclinationChart" height="300"></canvas>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p>Three dominant inclination bands emerge:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>50–55°</strong> (5,324 satellites): Starlink's primary shell at 53° accounts for the vast majority. This inclination provides coverage to most populated latitudes.</li>
      <li><strong>40–45°</strong> (3,744 satellites): Starlink's secondary V2 Mini shell, optimizing for mid-latitude capacity.</li>
      <li><strong>95–100°</strong> (2,803 satellites): Sun-synchronous orbits (SSO), heavily used by Earth-observation platforms and some communications constellations including OneWeb (87.9°) and imaging satellites.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Eccentricity Distribution</h3>
