    <h2>Key Takeaways &amp; Outlook</h2>

    <h3>1. Unprecedented Growth, Accelerating</h3>
    <p>The active satellite population has grown more than 10× since 2019 (1,414 → 14,875). Over 90% of the current fleet was launched in the last six years. If 2026 deployment continues at the 2025 pace, the catalog could surpass 18,000 active satellites by year-end.</p>

    <h3>2. Single-Operator Dominance Reshapes the Environment</h3>
    <p>Starlink's 10,083 satellites constitute 67.8% of all active objects. This concentration skews every metric — orbital regime shares, inclination distributions, mean motion profiles, and launch statistics. Regulators and space-situational-awareness providers must account for this when setting policy or evaluating collision risk.</p>

    <h3>3. LEO Congestion Is the Central Challenge</h3>
    <p>With 86.5% of active satellites in LEO and the vast majority clustered between 500–600 km altitude, conjunction rates and close-approach events will continue to rise. The 550 km shell is becoming the single most congested region in orbital history.</p>

    <h3>4. New Entrants Are Scaling Rapidly</h3>
    <p>Amazon's Kuiper (210 active), China's Hulianwang (159) and Qianfan (108) constellations are still in early deployment phases. Their planned fleet sizes (3,200+ for Kuiper; thousands for each Chinese constellation) will add substantial new populations to an already crowded LEO.</p>

    <h3>5. Policy Implications</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Spectrum management:</strong> Mega-constellations dominate specific frequency bands. Coordination between operators in the Ku/Ka bands is increasingly complex.</li>
      <li><strong>Debris mitigation:</strong> With fleet sizes exceeding 10,000, even a low per-satellite failure rate generates meaningful debris risk. Post-mission disposal compliance is critical.</li>
      <li><strong>Space traffic management:</strong> Automated conjunction assessment and collision-avoidance maneuvering are no longer optional — they are operational necessities at current fleet scales.</li>
      <li><strong>Equitable access:</strong> Orbital slots and spectrum are finite resources. The current "first-mover" dynamic risks foreclosing access for later entrants, including developing nations.</li>
    </ul>
